; ct.rules — the derived state category, given its own vocabulary.
;
; A model's meaning IS a category (kernel Appendix I.1): objects are the
; reachable situations, morphisms are move sequences, composition is
; concatenation, identities are "do nothing". `pol check` asks three questions
; about it — `possible`, `live`, `never` — and the interrogator extension is
; explicit that those three are the common case rather than the instrument
; (extension §2). This file is the instrument's vocabulary: the structure of
; that category, named, so a model can be asked what the three modalities have
; no spelling for.
;
; WHERE IT LIVES. It is a RULES library — it declares relations, never types —
; so `(load "ct.rules")` belongs in a .rules file and nowhere else. Loading it
; into a .pol model would be accepted and useless: nothing here is a declaration
; a model's universe can hold.
;
; THE COST, stated as stdlib.pol states its own. Eight relation names: a .rules
; file that loads this one gives up `reach`, `mutual`, `before`, `one-way`,
; `escapes`, `final-phase`, `moves` and `dead-end`, and may not redeclare any of
; them — which is exactly what the corpus's existing files do with the first,
; and why the migration is a deletion.

; 1. Hom-sets — the free category on the reachable graph
;
; WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE, and it is the plainest case in the file: every worked
; scenario's .rules file opens with these three lines, character for character.
; Ten copies of one definition is the thing a standard library exists to end.
;
; `reach S T` is the hom-set of the state category made a relation: non-empty
; exactly when some move sequence runs from S to T. The first rule is the
; identity morphism, the second is composition — the free category, stated as
; the two clauses that generate it.
;
; No `init` premise is needed and none is wanted: the built-ins range over
; REACHABLE situations only (§12.3), so the space the engine enumerated is
; already the space that exists.
(relation reach (Situation Situation))
(rule (reach S S) (situation S))
(rule (reach S T) (edge E S M) (reach M T))

; 2. Isomorphism — the maximal subgroupoid
;
; Two situations are isomorphic in the state category when each reaches the
; other, and `mutual` is that relation: an equivalence, whose classes a graph
; tool would call strongly connected components. The categorical name is the one
; worth keeping, because it says what the classes MEAN — inside one, nothing has
; been spent; every arrangement in it is recoverable from every other.
(relation mutual (Situation Situation))
(rule (mutual S T) (reach S T) (reach T S))

; 3. The posetal reflection — what a move costs
;
; Quotient the state category by §2 and what is left is a POSET: the same order,
; every cycle collapsed to a point. `before` is that order, strictly — S can
; become T, and T can never become S again.
;
; WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE. This is what most models are actually for, and it is
; the question the modalities cannot put. `live G` failing says a trap exists
; and prints one route into it; `before` is the whole trap structure, and it is
; asked WITHOUT naming a goal — which is what makes it answerable of a model
; nobody has yet written the claims for.
(relation before (Situation Situation))
(rule (before S T) (reach S T) (not (reach T S)))

; 4. One-way doors — irreversibility, per move
;
; §3 in the form an author can act on: a NAMED move, and the situation it leaves
; for good. §15 reports which moves can break a law; this reports which moves
; break a return. A model with no one-way rows is fully reversible, and that is
; worth being able to state rather than believe.
(relation one-way (Edge Situation Situation))
(rule (one-way E S T) (edge E S T) (not (reach T S)))

; 5. Final phases — where a model ends up
;
; A situation in a TERMINAL class of §3's poset. It may still move, perhaps
; forever, but everything it reaches reaches it back: nothing further is
; decided. Endgames, absorbed states, the emergency nobody can lift.
;
; `escapes` is scaffolding rather than vocabulary, and is named instead of
; hidden because it costs a name either way: a variable inside `not` must be
; bound elsewhere in the body (range restriction, extension §1), so "there is no
; T with (before S T)" has to become a relation before it can be negated.
(relation escapes (Situation))
(rule (escapes S) (before S T))
(relation final-phase (Situation))
(rule (final-phase S) (situation S) (not (escapes S)))

; 6. Dead ends
;
; A situation with no move at all — the degenerate final phase, a class of one
; object carrying nothing but its identity. §15 already reports these, and the
; reason to have them HERE is that a relation JOINS: "which one-way move leads
; straight into one" is then a query, instead of two reports read side by side.
(relation moves (Situation))
(rule (moves S) (edge E S T))
(relation dead-end (Situation))
(rule (dead-end S) (situation S) (not (moves S)))

; WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why — both limits are mechanical, and both are recorded
; so the next reader does not spend an afternoon rediscovering them.
;
; THE ARROW PROPERTIES OF A SCHEMA. An arrow being injective or surjective, a
; span being the whole pullback and not merely a sound part of it — these belong
; to this file by subject, and cannot be written in it. A rule reads an arrow by
; naming it inside a chain (`X.stands`), and a form substitutes only a path's
; ROOT segment (`Expander.subst`, and deliberately: it is why `maybe` sugars a
; whole declaration rather than part of one). So no template can be generic over
; the arrow, and each such question stays one rule per arrow, written where the
; arrow is known — the model's own .rules file. What the kernel refuses to a LAW
; here is a second, independent limit: §10.2 decides the right of `is`
; lexically, so two bound variables cannot be compared in a guard at all, which
; is why injectivity is a rules question and never an `equation`.
;
; A FORM TAKING A GUARD. `(satisfies R G)` — the situations where G holds — and
; its complement, the trap set for a stated goal, is the shape every scenario in
; the corpus writes by hand, and a guard datum does travel as a form slot. What
; stops it is the template check: a head in a form template must be a reserved
; word, a slot, or an earlier form (`Forms.check`), and the extension's built-in
; relations — `situation`, `edge`, `holds` — are none of the three. Reserving
; them would point the kernel's expander at an extension a conforming processor
; need not implement (`Forms.is_slot`'s own note), so the block is left standing.
; Naming a goal is model vocabulary in any case; only the plumbing is missing.
